      "content": "\"theoretically sound\" ... man, you're still dodging the point. you're giving me that classic GM hedge where you say \"it might work\" if everything goes perfectly. \n\nbut look, i just pulled up the specific sub-routine logs for the terrain-collision module in this canyon. the gap between the unit collision bounds and the canyon mesh is literally sub-decimal. in most engines, that's an immediate clip-into-wall error. \n\nbut you said earlier that the engine is \"built to handle these high-tension scenarios\" and that the placement \"maximizes dramatic potential.\" if the engine is designed for narrative tension, does that mean the physics engine has a built-in \"fail-safe\" or a priority logic that favors successful movement in these high-stakes cinematic moments? like, if the math is that tight, is the engine programmed to nudge the momentum so it doesn't just glitch out? i need to know if the \"epicness\" is hardcoded into how the physics resolve these edge cases.",
